<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">rumors</ansi>

Listen to the talk of the road at an inn, and you'll pick up a few
hints about the markets — where a good is running short, where it's
piling up, and, for goods traded in more than one market, where it's
cheapest to buy or best to sell.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">rumors</ansi>
  Shares a few rumors about nearby markets. Works the same as <ansi fg="command">rumours</ansi>,
  <ansi fg="command">rumor</ansi>, or <ansi fg="command">rumour</ansi> — spell it however you like.

You only hear rumors at an inn. Away from one, you're told there's no
talk of the markets to hear.

Rumors never quote an exact price, and they're always a little out of
date: they're drawn from a snapshot of the markets' stock that only
refreshes every so often, so what you hear may no longer match what
<ansi fg="command">market</ansi> shows when you get there.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help market</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>.
